# [DD-13b.W1.2] format_design.md §6.3, THE THREE-CONFIG FILE, with real
# pcrec flags in place of the design note's illustrative ones.
#
# ONE DEFINITION, THREE TARGETS, THREE PREFIXES, ONE NAME. That is the
# whole acceptance sentence for this step, and this file is the only
# place in the tree where all four halves of it are observable at once:
# `--source -o <dir>` writes three artifacts; each stamps its own
# `<prefix>`; all three stamp `rx_info.name == "level_filter"`; and
# run.sh builds all three and requires them to ANSWER IDENTICALLY on this
# block's own cases, which is §6.3's "identity between them is a free
# control" made mechanical.
#
# THE CONFIG CASCADE IS EXERCISED IN BOTH MECHANISMS: `from` (two configs
# inherit `baseline`'s own `pcrec` line, materialised once) and `with`
# (each target composes one config). The flags are chosen to be
# ANSWER-IDENTICAL on purpose — `--no-captures` on a group-free pattern,
# `--engine=dfa` on a pattern the DFA takes anyway, a raised size cap
# nothing approaches — because a config that changed an ANSWER would make
# the agreement control wrong rather than strict, and the control is the
# point.
config baseline
  pcrec --no-captures
config strict from baseline
  pcrec --engine=dfa
config big from baseline
  pcrec --max-emit-bytes=2000000

target log_base   = level_filter with baseline
target log_strict = level_filter with strict
target log_big    = level_filter with big

pattern error|warn|fatal
name level_filter
description the three targets are three builds of THIS block
m "an error here" 3 8
m "a fatal one" 2 7
n "nothing here"
